Every clock of the boundary modes runs at the modular rate $\kappa$, with period $\beta = 2\pi/\kappa = 8\pi M$. Precisely:
\begin{enumerate}
\renewcommand{\labelenumi}{(\roman{enumi})}
\item Call a flow \emph{intrinsic} when it is a one-parameter group of inner automorphisms of the boundary-mode algebra, with even generator, preserving the vector grade. The intrinsic flows of a Clifford boundary-mode algebra are rotor (bivector-generated) flows, quadratic in the pair operators.
\item Call an intrinsic flow a \emph{clock} when it is hyperbolic (its adjoint action on the mode operators semisimple with real eigenvalues) with a common rapidity across the modes it moves, the rapidity being the parameter the geometric normalisation converts to Schwarzschild time. The algebra fixes only the orbits, not the speed (Fact \claimref{scrambling.imported-modes-modular-inventory}'s reparametrisation clause), so a clock's parameter is normalised to the rapidity itself, not a multiple of it. For a single completed pair the bivector space is one-dimensional, its flow is the boost, and the conversion runs at the surface gravity $\kappa = 1/4M$.
\item A nonzero bivector generates a clock exactly when its adjoint action on the mode operators is semisimple with real eigenvalues, all nonzero ones of equal magnitude, which the normalisation to rapidity sets to one; if $\operatorname{ad}_X$ is semisimple with spectrum $\{+c, 0, -c\}$ then the $\pm c$ eigenspaces are isotropic and mutually dual for the neutral form of the boundary modes, so by Witt extension $X$ is $O(n,n)$-conjugate to $c$ times a sum of standard atom boosts; the geometric normalisation, being a property of the quadratic space rather than of a choice of modes, is invariant under that conjugation and converts every such flow at the one $\kappa$. Writing $E_{ij} = \psi_i^\dagger\psi_j$ for the gauge-invariant bilinears of the $n$-mode boundary space's mode operators $\psi_i$ (Proposition \claimref{scrambling.boundary-mode-space-matrix}, proved independently of this lemma), the cases below are illustrative rather than a partition. The diagonal $E_{ii}$ are the atoms' boosts (Fact \claimref{scrambling.imported-modes-modular-inventory}) and the equal-coefficient sum $\sum_i E_{ii}$ their simultaneous boost, all with adjoint eigenvalues $\pm 1$; the symmetric combinations $E_{ij} + E_{ji}$ are opposed pairs of boosts of the rotated modes $(\psi_i \pm \psi_j)/\sqrt{2}$, again $\pm 1$. The antisymmetric $E_{ij} - E_{ji}$ are compact and the strictly off-diagonal $E_{ij}$ nilpotent, neither carrying a rapidity; a mixed diagonal $\sum_i c_i E_{ii}$ with unequal $|c_i|$ has no common rapidity. And the pairing sector $c\,\psi_i^\dagger\psi_j^\dagger + d\,\psi_j\psi_i$, outside $\mathfrak{gl}(n)$ and completing the bivector algebra of the completed boundary mode space, of dimension $n(2n-1)$ against $\mathfrak{gl}(n)$'s $n^2$ (the $n$ hyperbolic pairs of Fact \claimref{tower.witt-index-quantum-capacity} make the quadratic space split, so that algebra is $\mathfrak{so}(n,n)$), is classified by the sign of one product, its adjoint eigenvalues being $\pm\sqrt{cd}$: hyperbolic for $cd > 0$ (a clock, at unit rapidity outright when $cd = 1$), compact for $cd < 0$, nilpotent for $cd = 0$.
\end{enumerate}
The boundary mode space therefore has many intrinsic flows and one clock rate: whichever clock is read, single-atom, rotated-pair, common, or paired, its period is $\beta$.

With the epoch fixed at $\beta$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item the counted three-volume of one epoch is $V = (8\pi M)\cdot\beta = 64\pi^2M^2$ in Planck units; restoring the length, $V = \beta^2\lP$: the coincidence with $\beta^2$ is a consequence of the framework's units, one visible power of $\lP$ marking the volume as geometric rather than purely modular;
\item the prediction $\nu = 1/16\pi$ holds if and only if the number of boundary modes registered per modular period is
\[
N_{\mathrm{epoch}} \;=\; \nu\,V \;=\; 4\pi M^2 \;=\; \SBH
\]
exactly as arithmetic: \emph{one boundary-mode fermion per four Planck cells of horizon area, per modular period}. The transcendental density becomes an integer-holographic statement, in the 1-nat convention and inheriting the per-mode $\ln 2$ normalisation of Remark \claimref{note.scrambling.density-factor-stated-full};
\item the naive one-mode-per-volume-cell reading ($\nu = 1$) overshoots it by exactly $16\pi$, which is precisely the horizon-area coefficient $A = 16\pi M^2$: the thinning the weighted completion must produce is the area coefficient itself. (a figure of the original draws the assembled counting geometry.)
\end{enumerate}

In Planck units, with $r_h = 2M$, $A = 4\pi r_h^2 = 16\pi M^2$, $\beta = 8\pi M$ and $\SBH = A/4$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] the slice count per unit Killing time is $8\pi M = 4\pi r_h = A/r_h$, at leading order in $r_c/2M$;
\item[(ii)] the slice count per modular period is $N_{\mathrm{slice}} = 64\pi^2 M^2$ and $N_{\mathrm{slice}}/\SBH = 16\pi$, at the same order;
\item[(iii)] $16\pi = (\beta/r_h) \times (A/\SBH) = 4\pi \times 4$, and the first factor is universal for Schwarzschild, $\beta/r_h = 2\pi/(\kappa r_h) = 4\pi$ with $\kappa = 1/(2r_h)$.
\end{enumerate}

Let a calibration principle be a rule assigning to the exceptional set $E = \{\tau = 0\} \times \R_t \times S^2$ a count of boundary-mode fermions per modular period. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(P1)] \emph{one fermion per Planck cell of the slice} returns $N_{\mathrm{epoch}} = 16\pi\,\SBH$, and the coefficient is scheme-bound: the slice count scales as $c_0^{3/2}$, so the proper-time scheme returns exactly $\tfrac32$ times the curvature scheme's count and the Kretschmann scheme $12^{1/4}$ times it, an irrational multiple;
\item[(P3)] \emph{one fermion per Planck cell of the sphere at horizon normalisation, once per modular period} returns $N_{\mathrm{epoch}} = A = 4\,\SBH$, independently of the cutoff scheme, because the horizon-normalised coframe on $E$ is exactly $(dt,\ r_h\,d\theta,\ r_h\sin\theta\,d\varphi)$ (Fact \claimref{scrambling.imported-collars-coframe-exceptional-set-helicity});
\item[(P2)] \emph{fundamentals per sphere aggregated over the epoch} breaks the $M$-scaling (Proposition \claimref{scrambling.per-slice-verification-two-failed-aggregations}).
\end{enumerate}

Write the epoch count as $N_{\mathrm{epoch}} = \kappa_c \times A \times 1$, horizon-normalised sphere cells times one tick per modular period, with $\kappa_c$ the dimensionless \emph{calibration residue}, a symbol distinct from the surface gravity $\kappa$ of the section ``Setting and imports''. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] in the 1-nat convention of this paper, $N_{\mathrm{epoch}} = \SBH$ gives $\kappa_c = \SBH/A = \tfrac14$;
\item[(ii)] in the capacity convention of the singularity paper, $N_{\mathrm{epoch}}\ln 2 = \SBH$ gives $\kappa_c = 1/(4\ln 2)$: one boundary mode per $4\ln 2$ Planck areas of horizon per modular period, which is one mode per Bekenstein-Mukhanov area quantum \cite{BekensteinMukhanov1995};
\item[(iii)] the two differ by exactly $\ln 2$, and only (ii) compares an entropy with an entropy: a fermionic mode carries at most $\ln 2$ nats, so the 1-nat convention credits each mode with more entropy than it can hold, and the exact ``$16\pi$'' of Theorem \claimref{scrambling.epoch-modular-period-two-unknowns-become} is a property of that convention, the physical thinning being $16\pi\ln 2$;
\item[(iv)] one boundary mode registers one helicity: by Fact \claimref{scrambling.imported-collars-coframe-exceptional-set-helicity} the two helicities of a massless species are two channels of the lift, so the capacity per boundary mode is $\ln 2$ and not $2\ln 2$, and $\kappa_c$ is $1/(4\ln 2)$, not $1/(8\ln 2)$.
\end{enumerate}

Let $(v, v^*)$ be a completed pair and $\sigma := v^*v - vv^*$. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\sigma^2 = 1$; in the hyperbolic atom $\Cl(1,1)$ with $v = \tfrac12(e_0 + e_1)$, $v^* = \tfrac12(e_0 - e_1)$, one has $\sigma = e_0 e_1$: the unit boost bivector of the plane (relabelling the pair reverses the orientation);
\item the rotor flow $R_\lambda := \exp(\lambda\sigma/2)$ acts by $R_\lambda\, v\, R_\lambda^{-1} = e^{-\lambda}v$ and $R_\lambda\, v^*\, R_\lambda^{-1} = e^{+\lambda}v^*$: the pair diagonalises the boost with opposite weights;
\item the spectral projectors $\tfrac12(1 \pm \sigma)$ are the occupation projectors $v^*v$ and $vv^*$;
\item in the Fock representation the boost generator is the shifted number operator, $\tfrac12\sigma = N - \tfrac12$;
\item the bivector space of $\Cl(1,1)$ is one-dimensional, so the boost flow is the unique one-parameter rotor flow of the atom \emph{up to reparametrisation of the flow parameter and orientation}: the algebra fixes the orbits of any rotor-implemented modular flow, but not its speed, which is exactly the normalisation Remark \claimref{note.hawking.what-now-proved-open-problem} (the section ``The state input: the I-state'') leaves open.
\end{enumerate}

For the Schwarzschild gravitational field $(\hb, \Omega)$ on flat spacetime (Definition \claimref{obj.synthesis.gtg-flat-spacetime-gauge-fields}), within the class of diagonal rescalings $r^{a_i}$ of the Schwarzschild coordinate coframe, demand that its derived cometric, read on the rescaled coframe, extend continuously to $E$. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item requiring a corank-one limit whose dying entry is the Killing-time entry, vanishing at first order, forces $(a_r, a_t, a_\theta) = (\tfrac12, 0, 1)$, uniquely: the degeneration-sheaf regime of Theorem \claimref{sheaf.schwarzschild-degeneration-boundary-tame};
\item requiring a nondegenerate limit forces $(a_r, a_t, a_\theta) = (\tfrac12, -\tfrac12, 1)$, uniquely: the exceptional regime of Proposition \claimref{sheaf.exceptional-algebra-black-hole}.
\end{enumerate}
The naming of the dying entry in (1) is not a free choice but is read off the gravitational field: the radical of the actual degeneration is the Killing-time covector (Theorem \claimref{sheaf.schwarzschild-degeneration-boundary-tame}(4)); without naming it, other slot assignments exist (for instance $(1, -\tfrac12, 1)$ sends the radial entry to zero at first order instead), so the forcing is per named profile. Converted to proper time $\tau_{\mathrm{p}} \propto r^{3/2}$, the exceptional weights are the Kasner exponents $(-\tfrac13, \tfrac23, \tfrac23)$ of Fact \claimref{sheaf.kasner-data-interior}, with $\sum p_i = \sum p_i^2 = 1$. This settles the Kasner-weights conjecture for Schwarzschild within the diagonal class: given the dying direction determined by the field, the weights of the anisotropic completion are not chosen, they are forced, and they are the Kasner exponents.

Let $\sigma = e_0e_1$ be the boost bivector of the hyperbolic atom in the radial plane, with $\sigma^{2} = +1$, and let $\Aa \cong \Mat_2(\C)$ be the mode algebra, the complexification of the atom.
\begin{enumerate}
\item The boost rotor $R_\lambda = \exp(\lambda\sigma/2)$ continues to imaginary rapidity $\lambda = i\theta$ as $R_{i\theta} = \exp(i\theta\sigma/2) = \cos(\theta/2) + i\sigma\sin(\theta/2)$, a unitary of $\Aa$ whose generator $i\sigma$ squares to $-1$; $R_{2\pi i} = -1$ and $R_{4\pi i} = +1$, so the rotor has period $4\pi$ and its conjugation action period $2\pi$.
\item On the two-dimensional module on which $v, v^{*}$ act as ladder operators, $R_{i\theta} = \operatorname{diag}(e^{-i\theta/2}, e^{i\theta/2})$: a rotation through $2\pi$ multiplies every spinor by $-1$, while its conjugation action on $\Aa$ is the identity.
\item The generator $i\sigma$ is not an element of the real algebra $\Cl(1,3)$. The dual member of the curvature-selected pair, $I\sigma_r = e_0e_1e_2e_3\,e_0e_1 = e_2e_3$, is the \emph{angular} plane: its rotor $\exp(\theta I\sigma_r/2)$ fixes $e_0$, $e_1$ and $\sigma_r$ and rotates $e_2$ into $e_3$, a rotation along the bifurcation sphere, not around it. The Euclidean rotation around the bifurcation sphere is therefore realised by no real rotor of $\Cl(1,3)$, and in particular not by the curvature-selected pair $\{\sigma_r, I\sigma_r\}$; it is realised by the unitary $R_{i\theta}$ of the complexified radial plane.
\end{enumerate}

Let $v_1, \dots, v_k, w_1, \dots, w_k \in V$ be a \emph{Witt basis} of their span: $B(v_i, v_j) = B(w_i, w_j) = 0$ and $B(v_i, w_j) = \tfrac12 \delta_{ij}$. Then in $\Cl(V, Q)$
\begin{equation}
v_i v_j + v_j v_i = 0, \qquad w_i w_j + w_j w_i = 0, \qquad v_i w_j + w_j v_i = \delta_{ij},
\end{equation}
the canonical anticommutation relations for $k$ modes (read $v_i$ as annihilators, $w_i$ as creators). The elements $N_i = w_i v_i$ are mutually commuting idempotents with $N_i + v_i w_i = 1$. In particular, for orthogonal hyperbolic planes $\operatorname{span}(e_i, e_{k+i})$ of $\R^{k,k}$, $v_i = \tfrac12(e_i + e_{k+i})$ and $w_i = \tfrac12(e_i - e_{k+i})$ form such a basis. The form need not be nondegenerate outside the span.
